# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-welcome-p6 = A button that could lose something, such as a bin, asks twice. The first click fills it with the highlight color and shows a tick; click again to go ahead. Leave it, and after three seconds it goes back as it was.

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-ring-keywords = launcher tray icons brightness tools apps applications start menu open program edge click favorites favorites all apps categories office games type search find filter

# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-ring-p3 = Click the sound, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi or battery icon in the middle and a round panel opens over the favorites: change the volume or the microphone, connect Bluetooth headphones, join a Wi-Fi network, or see how long the battery will last and how worn it is. The gear in the panel opens the full Settings page; the cross closes it.

# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-ring-p4 = Round the middle sit your favorite apps, with Settings, Apps and Folders always among them, and four tool circles drawn like apps: Screenshot (the camera), Clipboard, Notifications and the Magnifier. Click an app to open it, or to bring its window to the front. Programs that are open show in the ring of open windows round the favorites, not among the favorites.

# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 7.
settings-help-topics-ring-step7-1 = Click All apps in the middle. Every app that is not already a favorite appears round the outside, in pieces: Internet, Office, Graphics, Sound and video, Games and so on. A kind with no apps left has no piece.

# Title of the Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring".
settings-help-topics-favourites-title = Keeping your favorite apps in the ring

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-favourites-keywords = favorite favorites favorite favorites keep add remove right-click drag move order

# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p1 = Right-click any app, in the ring or among All apps, and choose Add to favorites. It then always sits round the middle of the ring. Right-click a favorite and choose Remove from favorites to take it away. The middle holds twelve.

# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p3 = To put your favorites in a different order, drag one with the mouse and drop it on another's place; it takes that place and the rest move round.

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Switching between windows" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-switcher-keywords = windows switch switcher alt tab open ring desktop show minimize clipboard copy paste history keyboard on screen

# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p1 = Open the ring and your open windows are in a ring round the favorites, one part for each window, with its program's icon and its name. Point at one and the middle shows a picture of that window; click it to bring that window to the front, or right-click it for minimize, maximize and close. Alt and Tab also brings each open window to the front in turn.

# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p3 = The clipboard is one of the four tool circles among the favorites in the ring, and opens as a round panel, like the sound or Wi-Fi ones. It shows what you copied lately, newest first: type in the filter to find one, click one to copy it again, and pin the ones you want kept for good. The small cross beside a line forgets just that one, handy for a password copied by mistake, and the bin at the foot forgets everything but the pinned ones, and asks twice. Choosing a line only puts it back on the clipboard: you paste it yourself, where you mean it to go, so nothing ever lands in a box you did not choose. Settings, Ring, Clipboard chooses when the rest is forgotten: each restart, after a day, a week or two weeks, or never.

# Help topic "Getting new apps": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-apps-step4-1 = To remove an app, find it in Apps and press the bin twice: the first press fills it with the highlight color and shows a tick, and the second removes it.

# Title of the Help topic "Picking a colour from the screen".
settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-title = Picking a color from the screen

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Picking a colour from the screen" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-keywords = eye dropper eyedropper color color pick picker hex code copy swatch screen

# Help topic "Picking a colour from the screen": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-p1 = The eye dropper reads the color of any single dot on the screen and copies its code, such as #FBC711, so you can paste it into a document or a design.

# Help topic "Picking a colour from the screen": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-eye-dropper-tip3 = Colors taken earlier stay in the list, so you can copy one again later without picking it again.

# Help topic "Having the screen read aloud": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-screen-reader-p1 = No screen reader is installed, so the switch in Settings, Appearance, under Accessibility, is grayed and says so.

# Title of the Help topic "Colours, icons and text".
settings-help-topics-look-title = Colors, icons and text

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Colours, icons and text" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-look-keywords = color colors color colors accent theme dark light mode sunshine bright font text icons cursor pointer look

# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-look-p2 = The highlight color is used for buttons, switches and wherever you are; pick one of the twenty colors or type your own.

# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-look-p4 = Under Icons, the Trindle Icons are circles: each app's own mark in white on a color of its own, the same in light and in dark. An app the set does not draw yet gets its own picture on a white circle, so it still fits in.

# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p1 = A theme is a whole look, colors, corners, sizes and room, kept in one file. Settings, Appearance, Theme shows every theme as a small picture drawn from its own colors. Trindle's own look, a deep blue night, is always first in the list and can never be removed. Midnight, which comes with Trindle, is black and gray with no blue in it, as Trindle's own look used to be.

# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p4 = A theme with both looks keeps them in the one file. Its dark values go in :root { "{" } { "}" } as usual, and its light ones in a second block, @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) { "{" } :root { "{" } { "}" } { "}" }, exactly as Trindle's own sheet does. Give the light block every color and shadow the dark look sets, or the dark ones show through in the light look. Settings then shows Dark and Light beside the theme's picture. A theme that says dark or light has that one look, is always drawn in it, and has no such buttons; one that does not say is taken to have both when it has a light block and to be dark when it has none. Other programs are told the look your theme is really drawn in, so a light theme makes them light.

# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p5 = A theme is safe to try, whoever made it. It is only ever read as a list of values, never run, and it cannot bring in another file or reach the internet. Only values Trindle knows are taken, each kept within its limits, so no theme can hide a button, a question or a warning, or make text too small to read. Words are kept clear enough to read whatever colors a theme gives, red stays red, and your highlight color stays your own. A theme changes only your own windows: the login screen always wears Trindle's own look.

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-theming-keywords = theme theming style sheet stylesheet css colors colors shadows corners rounder bigger text size buttons background page store look custom own retheme

# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-theming-p1 = Everything Trindle draws, its colors, shadows, corners, the room between things, the size of buttons and the size and weight of words, comes from one style sheet, written the way web pages are styled. You, or an AI you ask, can change any of it with a sheet of your own.

# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-theming-p4 = Nothing you write can break a window. Names Trindle does not know are ignored, a value it cannot read is replaced by Trindle's own, and sizes are kept within their limits. Colors are up to you: a color you set is left exactly as you wrote it, and Trindle moves its own words so they can still be read on a page, a card or a title bar of yours. A color set for the light look alone is yours in the light look only. A see-through color is measured over black and over white, as what shows through it is not known; where your own colors leave nothing that can be read on all of them, Trindle's words go as far as they can, to black or to white. Three things stay readable whatever a sheet says: red words that tell of a failure, the words on your highlight color, and the boxes that ask for a password, which are always drawn in Trindle's own look. When colors you set fall short of what can be read, Settings, Appearance says "Some colors are hard to read." and leaves them as you wrote them. If words you set yourself become hard to read, delete the file and Trindle's own look comes back at once. The file must be an ordinary file of words, at most 256 KB; one that is not is left unused, and Settings, Appearance says so.

# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-theming-tip6 = A color set in your sheet with --trindle-highlight wins over the highlight color chosen in Settings, Appearance.

# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-files-p6 = Press the space bar on a file to look at it without opening it, and again to close it. F4 opens a terminal in the folder you are in. Right-click a folder or a file and choose Pin to the ring to keep it round the middle of the ring with your favorite apps, where one click opens it; right-click it there to unpin it. Right-click pictures to resize or rotate them. A file's Properties can give it a small emblem, and a folder's can share it with other computers on your home network: the first time, Folders puts Samba on, which asks for your password, and sharing works once the computer has restarted. In the list view, right-click the column headings to add music, photo and PDF details.

# Help topic "Battery, sleep and shutting down": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-power-p5 = To log out, restart or shut down, open the ring and click the power button in its middle. The choices cover the middle. Lock and Screensaver happen at once; Suspend, Hibernate, Log out, Restart and Shut down ask twice, filling with the highlight color and showing a tick at the first click, so a stray click never ends anything. The cross goes back.

